abstract: 'Reproducibility software and research data accompanying the manuscript ''When predictive fit and screening utility diverge: choosing frequency or particle size in roadside-soil magnetometry''. The study is a secondary analysis of an archived 44-sample roadside-soil campaign along the Amman-Airport Highway, Jordan, including 43 complete fine/coarse magnetic pairs and nine measured metal concentrations. The materials support examination of prediction error, held-out screening recovery, nested measurement selection, frequency-representation sensitivity, block-deletion sensitivity and conditional resource accounting. Included source data and executable workflows are intended for reproducing and checking the reported analyses. The execution report supplied with the release records the checks actually completed and their scope. The archive does not establish independent geographic validation. Original software is licensed under MIT; author-owned data and documentation are licensed under
  CC BY 4.0, as specified in LICENSING.md. Third-party materials retain their original terms.'
